Declare the project, so cmake compiles the assembly instead of ignoring it.

libunwind's CMakeLists.txt has no project() call and no enable_language(). It
is written to be included from LLVM's runtimes/ build, which declares the
languages for it. Configured directly -- which is the only way to build it
against a sysroot this tree controls -- cmake has no ASM language enabled.

The failure is silent, and that is the whole reason this patch exists rather
than a workaround. src/CMakeLists.txt lists the two assembly files:

    set(LIBUNWIND_ASM_SOURCES
        UnwindRegistersRestore.S
        UnwindRegistersSave.S)

With no ASM language, cmake does not error on a source it cannot compile; it
drops it. libunwind then builds, installs, and produces an archive with no
__unw_getcontext in it, which is the function that captures the register state
every unwind starts from. Nothing says so until something links against it.

project() rather than enable_language(ASM) alone: the file also expects
CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER to have been probed, and a bare enable_language leaves
PROJECT_SOURCE_DIR unset, which the install rules read.

Verified: with this patch cmake reports `Building ASM object
src/CMakeFiles/unwind_static_objects.dir/UnwindRegistersSave.S.o`, the archive
defines __unw_getcontext, _Unwind_Backtrace and _Unwind_GetIP, and a program
built with -fno-sanitize-trap=cfi -fsanitize-recover=cfi links against it and
prints the file and line of the violation.

Not upstreamable: upstream's answer is to build through runtimes/, and that
entry point needs the full LLVM source tree.

--- a/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -3,6 +3,8 @@
 #===============================================================================
 
 cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.20.0)
+
+project(libunwind LANGUAGES C CXX ASM)
 
 set(LLVM_COMMON_CMAKE_UTILS "${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/../cmake")
 
